{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And you shall be as Elohim, knowing good and evil.</b> It would appear that before the sin of man he had no faculty to know and distinguish between good and evil; if so, we find a sinner rewarded, that he acquired through his sin a faculty he did not possess at the outset. And what is evident to me from this is that the intention was not that man should be without intellect like the beasts and animals, which do not know good and evil — but this is not so, for the meaning of והייתם כאלהים is as its Targum renders: ״ותהון כרברביא חכמין בין טב לביש״ — 'and you shall be like great ones, wise between good and evil.' And certainly the intention of Creation regarding man was that he be a possessor of intellect, and this is ״ויברא אלהים את האדם בצלמו בצלם אלהים ברא אותו״, and the צלם is the intellect, and by reason of the intellect He commanded him, as it says ״ויצו ה׳ אלהים״; and the speech of HaShem, blessed be He, and His commandments are not for beasts, nor for one who has no intellect. With the intellect man distinguishes between truth and falsehood; but the knowledge of good and evil is not among intelligibles, for one does not say 'the spherical heavens are beautiful and the flat earth is ugly,' but rather one says of them truth and falsehood." "<b>And behold.</b> Before the sin his entirety was divine-intellectual; he was not occupied with physical things and did not know them, and nakedness was not in his eyes a disgraceful thing, but like the rest of the limbs. And when he sinned and acquired the power of desire and was drawn after the pleasures of the body, and saw that the tree was good for food and that it was a desire to the eyes, etc., he was punished immediately: for he was stripped of the intellectual apprehension and clothed in physical desire and the nature of the qualities of beauty and ugliness. Therefore it says והייתם כאלהים ידעי טוב ורע, and it did not say 'knowers of truth and falsehood' or 'apprehenders of truth and falsehood.' And observe what it says: ״ותפקחנה עיני שניהם וידעו״ — and it did not say ויראו, 'and they saw,' for what they saw now they had seen before. But the intention is that he grasped a new matter: it was disgraceful to him now and was not disgraceful to him at first. And his punishment was measure for measure (מדה כנגד מדה), because he transgressed the mean and turned his heart to the pleasures of the world, to eat what was forbidden him and to seek the desire of the body like the beasts; therefore He punished him with the food of beasts and made him like them, as it is written: ״ואכלת את עשב השדה״. This is the language of the Rambam."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:51.021894901Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}